{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfun2bt57Jpyz31PZsUK6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No. A Cloudflare Worker can't listen on an arbitrary port for inbound raw TCP. Workers are only invoked by HTTP(S) requests (including WebSocket upgrades) and other event triggers such as Cron, Queues, and Email.\n\nWhat Workers *can* do is open **outbound** TCP connections with the `connect()` API from `cloudflare:sockets`. That means a Worker can talk to Postgres, and **Hyperdrive** adds connection pooling and caching for that.\n\nFor an inbound TCP proxy on 5432, you'd typically use **Cloudflare Spectrum**, which proxies TCP/UDP to an origin server rather than to a Worker. Another option is to run the proxy on a normal VM or container host, possibly behind Cloudflare Tunnel."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2255,"cache_read_input_tokens":535,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2255},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"59dc109d-83da-48fa-9f74-446183fdfe54","uuid":"0e03b273-149d-4919-8e10-f517ba201bd1","timestamp":"2026-10-11T03:56:53.247Z","request_id":"req_011Cfun2bhASRHnk6jhZvZN8"}
